\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-0.3em}

We identified \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse} as the root cause of MoE adapter failure on time series foundation models. Eight causal controls---including opposite entropy trajectories on identical data (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast})---isolate the mechanism, and a mutual-information decomposition predicts where it applies ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $n{=}9$). Because the diagnosis pinpoints the cause, the fix principle is clear: \textbf{RR-MoA} routes on raw, pre-normalization input, achieving \textbf{54/54 wins} and a Frozen Paradox where frozen adapters outperform full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$. The diagnosis is further validated by its actionability: restoring raw-signal access to experts matches or beats DLinear on $107/123$ cells across six backbones.

\textbf{Practitioner guideline.} Compute $R(\mathcal{D})$: in our 9 LTSF benchmarks, values above $0.5$ reliably predict raw routing helps (6/6 datasets, $27$--$77\%$ improvement). Below $0.2$ the prediction is one-sided: Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, $\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$) is correctly predicted to not benefit, but Solar ($R{=}0.06$, $\Delta{=}{-}32.9\%$) does benefit because the Conv1d router exploits temporal shape patterns beyond location-scale (SSR ablation, Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}). $R(\mathcal{D})$ thus captures a sufficient (not necessary) condition for raw routing to help; high $R$ is a strong positive predictor, while low $R$ does not rule out gains from richer temporal structure. \textbf{Limitations:} Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe} is untested; Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} covers linear backbones only (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory} confirms the mechanism in full Transformers); $R(\mathcal{D})$ uses first moments and may miss higher-order routing signals (Solar outlier).

