\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-0.3em}

A natural mixture-of-expert adapter for instance-normalized time series foundation models does not work, and the reason is structural rather than algorithmic: the normalization layer the backbone relies on for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics that a downstream router would have used to tell input regimes apart. Once that is recognized, every part of the empirical picture follows. Standard MoE rescue mechanisms cannot help, because they target the router's optimization rather than its input. Hidden-state routing collapses on instance-normalized backbones but not on LayerNorm or T5-style ones. Frozen-backbone adapters beat full fine-tuning, because unfreezing only deepens the co-adaptation feedback. And the simplest possible fix---routing on the raw, pre-normalization input---wins consistently across datasets, freeze levels, backbones, and modalities. We see RR-MoA less as a new architecture than as the minimal architectural concession that the diagnosis forces.

The diagnosis is also actionable: a tractable signal-ratio statistic computed on the raw data predicts which datasets benefit from raw routing without training any model, and the same principle generalises beyond MoE adapters to architectural choices like Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} that restore raw-signal access at the expert level.

\textbf{Limitations.} Our analysis covers RevIN-style instance normalization and the five rescue families that dominate current MoE practice; whether other normalization schemes (e.g., the FFN-internal experts of Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe}) or yet-untried rescue mechanisms exhibit the same failure mode is open. The MI bound is exact, but the signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D})$ uses first and second moments only and can miss higher-order routing signals (Solar is the one such case in our suite). Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is proved for linear backbones; the trajectory experiments confirm the mechanism in full Transformers but the formal extension is left to future work.

