573	\section{Conclusion}
574	\vspace{-0.3em}
575	
576	A natural MoE adapter on instance-normalized time series foundation models fails for structural reasons: the normalization the backbone relies on for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics a downstream router needs. Once recognized, the empirical picture follows---rescue mechanisms targeting the router's optimization cannot help, hidden-state routing collapses only on instance-normalized backbones, frozen adapters beat full fine-tuning, and routing on the raw pre-normalization input wins across datasets, freeze levels, backbones, and modalities. The signal-ratio statistic predicts where the fix helps without training, and the same principle motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}, which restores raw-signal access at the expert level. \textbf{Limitations:} our analysis covers RevIN-style instance normalization and the five dominant rescue families; $R(\mathcal{D})$ uses second-order moments and can miss higher-order signals (Solar); Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is proved for linear backbones, with the Transformer extension left to future work.
