{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} RR-MoA is not specific to MOMENT. It wins all 21 MOMENT-small and MOMENT-large configurations ($27$--$81\%$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_backbone}) and 18/18 Moirai configurations ($3$--$31\%$), the latter showing that raw-signal routing helps even on LayerNorm-only backbones whose hidden states already retain routing information. On Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe}, which already contains sparse internal experts, RR-MoA still improves all 6 datasets by $37$--$90\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}): adapter-level and backbone-internal routing operate on complementary axes. The two negative controls predicted by Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}---Chronos and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl}, neither of which uses instance normalization---show no collapse and no RR-MoA gain (4/18 wins, within chance; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).}

{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Sparse routing and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
Top-2 sparse routing retains most of the dense gain at a $+10\%$ latency cost (Appendix~\ref{app:topk}); the advantage extends to imputation ($7/8$ datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}) and is stable across forecast horizons $H{\in}\{96{,}192{,}336{,}720\}$ (24/24 wins; Table~\ref{tab:horizon}). The companion \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} variant (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}) extends this to all 6 datasets across 5 backbones and 4 horizons (Table~\ref{tab:residual_ia_plus_mhcb}).}

