\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-0.4em}

A natural MoE adapter on instance-normalized TSFMs fails for structural reasons: the normalization the backbone relies on for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics a downstream router needs. Standard rescue mechanisms target the router's optimization rather than its input and cannot help; routing on the raw, pre-normalization input does, and the same principle predicts the Frozen Paradox and motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}. The signal-ratio statistic identifies where the fix applies without training. \textbf{Limitations:} we cover RevIN-style normalization and the five dominant rescue families; $R(\mathcal{D})$ uses second-order moments and can miss higher-order signals (Solar); Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is proved for linear backbones, with the Transformer extension left to future work.

